## How it works

These four steps are the same for every repository. What happens after your first push depends on whether the repository is public or private.

<HowItWorks />

## Set up

Paste the setup prompt into your coding agent, or run the three commands yourself and give Claude Code one sentence. The first time the skill runs, it writes the config from what it finds in the repository, checks that the app records the same way twice, and writes the first clip. On the example app, that first run took [six to twelve minutes and one or two review rounds](/start/authoring/).

We test the skill with Claude Code. It is plain Markdown, so another agent that can run commands in your repository can follow it, but we have not run one end to end yet.

<Setup />

## The script is the clip

Each clip is a TypeScript file in the repository. Your agent writes it, and you can read it, change it and review it in a pull request like any other code. Recording it needs no agent and no model: CI runs the file as it is. The video beside this one is what CI recorded from it on the last push to `main`, and the highlighted line is the one running.

<Take base="{{clips}}" />

## What happens when you change the app

<Ledger />

Every push to `main` runs `npx versioncam check` against the example app. The left panel shows the latest run. For the right panel, we renamed one button's test id, changed nothing else, and ran the same check once.

<Runs base="{{clips}}" />

## Why a recording comes out the same every time

<dl class="vc-facts not-content">
  <div>
    <dt>Authored time</dt>
    <dd>The recorder controls the page's clock and moves it one frame at a time. While a query is slow or a spinner is showing, the recorder waits with the clock stopped, so the wait never appears in the video. <a href="/how-it-works/overview/">How it works</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Real input</dt>
    <dd>The recorder moves a real mouse along a planned path, generated from a seed, so hover and press states fire as they would for a person. The cursor you see in the video is drawn afterwards, at the output resolution. <a href="/reference/dsl/">The clip DSL</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Same bytes</dt>
    <dd>Record a clip twice on one machine and you get the same file, byte for byte. Before the Claude Code skill writes a clip for your app, it runs <code>versioncam stability</code>, which records a three-second clip twice and checks that both come out the same. <a href="/how-it-works/determinism/">Determinism</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Write a clip with one sentence</dt>
    <dd>In Claude Code, <code>/versioncam "…"</code> writes the clip and records it. Then a separate reviewer agent checks the frames. When the clip needs another try, a new reviewer checks it. You don't need an API key: it uses the Claude Code session you already have. <a href="/start/authoring/">Authoring</a></dd>
  </div>
</dl>

## After CI: version.cam

The recorder and the GitHub Action are free. They run in your CI, which already has your app and your secrets. version.cam is where the recordings go after CI has made them.

